This is a classic logical trap: if you define the "problem" as the entire universe of risks, then the "solution" (the insurance policy) must cover everything, leaving no room for the insurer to exclude anything.

In reality, the "problem" is only the specific, quantifiable risks the insurer is willing to underwrite. Therefore, the "solution" is a policy that covers *only* those defined risks, not the whole universe.

The key insight is that the scope of the problem determines the scope of the solution. If you try to solve for all possible risks, you end up with a policy that covers nothing, because no insurer will accept unlimited liability.

Thus, the correct approach is to define the problem narrowly enough that the solution (the policy) can actually cover it. This means identifying specific, manageable risks and constructing a policy around those, rather than attempting to insure against an undefined, all-encompassing set of possibilities.

The fundamental principle is that the solution must be tailored to the specific problem, not the hypothetical universe of all possible problems. By narrowing the problem definition, you create a feasible solution space where insurance becomes a viable and effective tool.

In summary, the solution (insurance) is only possible when the problem (risk exposure) is clearly defined and limited to what is insurable. Attempting to solve for everything leads to a paradox where no solution exists.
=== end ===
